<Docs slug="pick-a-folder">
  <p>
    The first time you open LightPhotos, the home page has one main button:
    <strong>Open Folder</strong>. Pick the folder holding the shoot you want
    to work on. LightPhotos scans it and fills the grid right away &mdash;
    and there is no import step, nothing to copy into a library. The files
    stay exactly where they are. Once you have added a folder, the home page
    lists your folders on the left instead; click one to open it.
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>The web build asks for permission.</strong> Your browser wants
      confirmation before any page may read a folder. Grant it for the folder
      you picked; that is the only access LightPhotos gets, and it covers one
      folder, not your whole drive.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep several folders at hand.</strong> Click
      <strong>Add Folder</strong> above the Folders list on the left, or
      press <kbd>Cmd+O</kbd>. Each folder stays in the list; click one to
      show its photos. To take a folder off the list, right-click it and
      choose <strong>Remove Folder from LightPhotos</strong>. Its photos and
      edits stay on disk.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep separate lists.</strong> The Folders list belongs to the
      open catalog, named in the header. Make another catalog to keep, say,
      client work apart from family photos &mdash; see
      <a href="/docs/catalogs.html">catalogs</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pick up where you left off.</strong> The desktop app reopens
      the last folder, photo and view when it starts. The web app lists your
      folders again at startup; click one and the browser asks for access
      again.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your settings live next to your photos.</strong> Ratings, labels
      and edits are written into a <code>.lightphotos</code> folder inside the
      folder you opened. Move the folder and the edits travel with it; delete
      that folder and you lose the edits, never the photos. The catalog
      keeps only a copy of the ratings, flags and labels, so nothing
      somewhere else holds your work hostage.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Only some files show up.</strong> The grid lists the formats
      LightPhotos can open &mdash; see
      <a href="/docs/formats.html">supported formats</a> for the full set and
      for what else gets written to disk.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The scan happens in the background. Thumbnails decode as they scroll into
    view, so you can start rating the first frames while the rest of the folder
    is still being worked out &mdash;
    <a href="/docs/browse.html">how browsing works</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
